\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Inverse-CDF sampling of a truncated Gaussian on $[0.45,1]$ ($\mu =0.93$, $\sigma =0.08$). (a) The truncated density is the Gaussian restricted to the interval and divided by the mass $Z$ inside it, so it is higher than the parent density. (b) A uniform level $u$ is mapped to $z=F_d^{-1}(u)$. (c) The samples follow $q_d$, and every sample lies in the interval.}}{30}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:inverse_cdf}{{3}{30}{Inverse-CDF sampling of a truncated Gaussian on $[0.45,1]$ ($\mu =0.93$, $\sigma =0.08$). (a) The truncated density is the Gaussian restricted to the interval and divided by the mass $Z$ inside it, so it is higher than the parent density. (b) A uniform level $u$ is mapped to $z=F_d^{-1}(u)$. (c) The samples follow $q_d$, and every sample lies in the interval}{figure.3}{}}
